---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import '../../styles/global.css'
const pageName = "projects"
---

<script>
for(let i = 0; i < document.querySelectorAll('project').length; i++){
  document.querySelectorAll('project')[i].addEventListener('click', (e) => {
    if (e.target.classList.contains('link-container'))  return;
    if(document.querySelectorAll('project')[i].classList.contains('out')){
      document.querySelectorAll('project')[i].classList.remove('out')
      document.querySelectorAll('project')[i].classList.add('in')
        
    }
    else {
        document.querySelectorAll('project')[i].classList.remove('in')
        document.querySelectorAll('project')[i].classList.add('out')
        deactivateAllProjects(i);
        
    }
  })

    
}

function deactivateAllProjects(sparedProject){
    for(let i = 0; i < document.querySelectorAll('project').length; i++){
        if(i !== sparedProject && document.querySelectorAll('project')[i].classList.contains('out')){
            document.querySelectorAll('project')[i].classList.remove('out')
            document.querySelectorAll('project')[i].classList.add('in')
        }
    }
}


</script>
<Layout pageName={pageName}>


<h2 style="font-family: var(--font-indie); margin-left: 0.5em;">What I've been working on...</h2>

<project>
    <project-row>
        <project-title>
            2011 Macbook Air Ubuntu Server
        </project-title>
        <project-expand>
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-chevron-right-icon lucide-chevron-right"><path d="m9 18 6-6-6-6"/></svg>
        </project-expand>
    </project-row>
    <project-description>
        I didn't want to pay for cloud hosting services 
        so I decided to repurpose an ancient Macbook Air into a server for all my homelab needs.
    </project-description>
    <project-content-wrapper>
        <project-content>
            <h3>Config</h3>
            <p>
            
                I installed Ubuntu Server (22.04.05 LTS) on my 2011 Macbook. Then, I set it up to run websites and web apps. In total, estimated costs are $35/year with it running 24/7. 
                Using Caddy DuckDNS to keep the IP records up to date I'm able to easily host websites & web apps.
                If you haven't tried creating your own server yet, but have been thinking about it I highly recommend you to try it out! Not only did I learn a lot about Linux, but I also learnt a lot about networking, dns records, and being able to host your own stuff with your own hardware is freaking awesome. 
            </p>
            <h3>Hosting a Node.js Website</h3>
            <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: row; flex-wrap:wrap; row-gap: 1em; column-gap: 1em;">
                <div class="blob">bigwb.isaacf.me</div>
                <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center;">
                      <svg style="stroke: var(--text-highlight);" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-move-right-icon lucide-move-right"><path d="M18 8L22 12L18 16"/><path d="M2 12H22"/></svg>
                      <p style="font-size: var(--font-smallest)"> CNAME</p>
                </div>
              
                <div class="blob">isaacf.duckdns.org</div>
                <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center;">
                      <svg style="stroke: var(--text-highlight);" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-move-right-icon lucide-move-right"><path d="M18 8L22 12L18 16"/><path d="M2 12H22"/></svg>
                      <p style="font-size: var(--font-smallest)"> A Record</p>
                </div>
                <div class="blob">37.120.24.5</div>
                <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center;">
                      <svg style="stroke: var(--text-highlight);" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-move-right-icon lucide-move-right"><path d="M18 8L22 12L18 16"/><path d="M2 12H22"/></svg>
                      <p style="font-size: var(--font-smallest)"> Caddy </p>
                </div>
                <div class="blob">localhost:3001</div>
            </div>
        </project-content>
    </project-content-wrapper>

</project>


<project>
    <project-row>
        <project-title>
            Weatherbuoy
        </project-title>
       <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: row; column-gap: 2em; align-items: center;">
        <a target="_blank" href="https://weatherbuoy-production.up.railway.app/" class="link-container">
            <svg style="pointer-events: none; color: var(--text-highlight)" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-link-icon lucide-link"><path d="M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 0 0-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71"/><path d="M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71"/></svg>
        </a>
        <project-expand>
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-chevron-right-icon lucide-chevron-right"><path d="m9 18 6-6-6-6"/></svg>
        </project-expand>
       </div>

    </project-row>
    <project-description>
        Weatherbuoy is a passion project me and a friend have been working on.
        We aim to replace the current outdated crusty PHP site that tracks the water temperature of our favorite lake in Berlin "Schlachtensee" with a more modern, user-friendly, and interesting website. 
    </project-description>
    <project-content-wrapper>
        <project-content>
            <h3>
                Design & Animation
            </h3>
            <p>While my friend Lars builds the actual machine that tracks the water temperature, I've been working on the front & back ends of the website.
            This was the first project where I used a dedicated animation framework (<a href="https://animejs.com/">anime.js</a>) in order to add unique effects to the design such as bubbles, a wave effect, and even some fish! 
            This was also the first time I ever used Figma to properly plan out a website's design before starting to code.
            We are far from done, but we hope that by the end of 2026 we'll have a weatherbuoy floating in Schlachtensee.</p>
            
        </project-content>
    </project-content-wrapper>

</project>
<project>
    <project-row>
        <project-title>
            Cosmos
        </project-title>
        <project-expand>
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-chevron-right-icon lucide-chevron-right"><path d="m9 18 6-6-6-6"/></svg>
        </project-expand>
    </project-row>
    <project-description>
        Cosmos is a work-in-progress open source 
        google drive alternative powered by Nuxt & Supabase. Cosmos turns file sharing into a more social experience by promoting sharing through friends instead of just links.
    </project-description>
    <project-content-wrapper>
        <project-content>
            <h3>Inspiration</h3>
            <p>I was motivated to create Cosmos because I wanted to have my own file storage interface 
                that I could potentially host on my own server that was secure, private, and easy to use. I also wanted to learn about networking, 
                authentication, and how file upload & downloads are handled. Also once it was up and running I wanted to be able to share files with friends and family and let them upload stuff as well. 
            </p>
            <h3>The Work</h3>
            <p>
                I built Cosmos using Nuxtv3, and used Supabase to handle authentication and database stuff. For the UI I used a mix of NuxtUI's premade components, and hand-drawn icons! 
                The big challenges when it came to Cosmos was the architecture behind file & especially folder upload/download. For a simple small file it was simple, but things got complicated when the file was big or someone uploaded a folder with hundreds or even thousands of files. 
                In order to solve these issues I needed to implement systems to split up the uploads in chunks, which would eleviate the load on the server, and I also needed to implement a cleanup service that would keep track of uploads, and regularly clean up those that failed due to network interrupts or other unexpected interruptions.
            </p>
            <h3>Reality</h3>
            <p>Eventually I got a barebones version of Cosmos running, and even shared it with some people to test it out. The file sharing functionality worked well, but there were still quite a few bugs lying around. There was also a lot of room for improvement, and I realized that the infrastructure required for hosting a virtual drive service is quite expensive.
            I also needed quick wifi, which was not happening any time soon due to me living in Germany so I realized that I should put the project on hold until I'm in a better place physically where I could actually realistically host Cosmos for others to use! 
            Thus Cosmos is not being worked on at the moment, but I do intend to continue working on it one day and create a finished product. 
            </p>
        </project-content>
    </project-content-wrapper>
</project>
<project>
    <project-row>
        <project-title>
            isaacf.me
        </project-title>
        <project-expand>
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-chevron-right-icon lucide-chevron-right"><path d="m9 18 6-6-6-6"/></svg>
        </project-expand>
    </project-row>
    <project-description>
        Why I built my personal website using the <a href="https://astro.build">astro</a> web framework.
    </project-description>
    <project-content-wrapper>
        <project-content>
            <h3>Markdown Collections</h3>
            <p> AstroJS is built for blog-focused sites. I thought their built-in markdown support was really neat, and I wanted to utilize it for the blogs on my website. For example, I built my food blog by defining a markdown file for each food review, and put it under src/content/foodblog. I defined metada that is attached to each food review in my content config file, and then astro automatically provided the collection of markdown files as a variable 
                I can use in my code. So I mapped each food review file to an actual site under the /food directory, therefore I never need to write any new HTML, JS, or CSS when adding a food review, just have to write the review and that's all!
            </p>
            <h3>Deploying on Railway</h3>
            <p>Getting my astro site production ready was surprisingly easy. All I had to do was add the AstroJS Node module which allowed me to build my website as a traditional client-server website with a Express.js backend. Then I created a Dockerfile that copies the important contents, builds the client-server site, and then runs the Express.js server.</p>
        </project-content>
    </project-content-wrapper>
</project>

</Layout>
